  {
    "id" : "SC1053",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Disallow Semicolons Directly After Else",
    "description" : "This rule enforces that semicolons should not be placed directly after the 'else' keyword in shell scripts.",
    "explanation" : "## Semicolons directly after `else` are not allowed. Just remove it.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\nif mycommand; then echo \"True\"; else; echo \"False\"; fi\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\nif mycommand; then echo \"True\"; else echo \"False\"; fi\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\n`else` keywords should not be followed by semicolons. It's not valid shell syntax.\n\nYou can follow them directly with a line break or another command.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nNone\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1053)",
