  {
    "id" : "SC1055",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty Code Blocks",
    "description" : "Detects empty code blocks such as functions or loops that contain no commands.",
    "explanation" : "## You need at least one command here. Use `true;` as a no-op.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\nsubmitbug() {\n  # TODO: Implement me\n}\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\nsubmitbug() {\n  # TODO: Implement me\n  true\n}\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nShellCheck found an empty code block. This could be an empty function as shown, a loop with an empty body, or similar.\n\nSh/bash does not allow empty code blocks. Insert at least one command. If you don't want the block to do anything, `true` (aka `:`) is a good no-op.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nNone\n\n### Related resources:\n* [StackOverflow: Why cannot I define an empty function in shell?](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/39307615/why-cannot-i-define-an-empty-function-in-shell)\n\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1055)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "SC1056",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Require Semicolon or Newline Before Closing Brace in Shell Functions",
    "description" : "Ensures that a closing brace `}` in shell script functions is preceded by a semicolon or newline to be recognized correctly as the end of a command group.",
    "explanation" : "## Expected a `}`. If you have one, try a `;` or `\\n` in front of it.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\nbar() { echo \"hello world\" }\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/bash\nbar() { echo \"hello world\";}\n```\n### Rationale:\n\n`}` is only recognized as the end of a command group when it's a separate token.\n\nIf it's not a separate token, like in the problematic example, it will be considered a literal character, as if writing `echo \"foo}\"` with quotes, and therefore usually cause a syntax error.\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1056)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "SC1057",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce presence of 'do' in loops",
    "description" : "This rule checks for missing 'do' statements in shell loop constructs such as 'while' loops.",
    "explanation" : "## Did you forget the `do` for this loop?\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\nwhile read -r line\n  echo $line\ndone\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\nwhile read -r line\ndo\n  echo $line\ndone\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nShellCheck found a loop that appears to be missing its `do` statement. Make sure the loop syntax is correct.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nNone.\n\n### Related resources:\n\n* Help by adding links to BashFAQ, StackOverflow, man pages, POSIX, etc!\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1057)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "SC1058",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce presence of 'do' in shell loops",
    "description" : "This rule checks that shell loop constructs include the required 'do' keyword.",
    "explanation" : "## Expected `do`.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\nfor file in *\n  echo \"$file\"\ndone\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\nfor file in *\ndo\n  echo \"$file\"\ndone\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nShellCheck has found a loop that appears to be missing a `do` statement. In the problematic code, it was simply forgotten.\n\nVerify that the `do` exists, and that it's in the correct place.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nNone\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1058)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "SC1059",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Disallow Semicolon Directly After 'do'",
    "description" : "This rule prohibits placing a semicolon immediately after the 'do' keyword in shell loops.",
    "explanation" : "## Semicolon is not allowed directly after `do`. You can just delete it.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\nwhile true; do; true; done\n\nwhile true;\ndo;\n  true;\ndone;\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\nwhile true; do true; done\n\nwhile true;\ndo\n  true;\ndone;\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nSemicolon `;` is not allowed directly after a `do` keyword. Follow it directly with either a command or a linefeed as shown in the example.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nNone.\n\n### Related resources:\n\n* Help by adding links to BashFAQ, StackOverflow, man pages, POSIX, etc!\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1059)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "SC1060",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Empty do Clauses in Shell Scripts",
    "description" : "Disallows empty do clauses in shell scripting loops or blocks; requires a no-op command like 'true' or ':'.",
    "explanation" : "## Can't have empty do clauses (use `true` as a no-op)\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\ndone\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\nfor i in 1 2 3; do\n  true\ndone\n```\n\n### Rationale:\n\nAn empty `do ... done` block is not valid.\nUse `true` or `:` if you need no command at all.\n\n### Exceptions:\n\nNone.\n\n### Related resources:\n\n* Help by adding links to BashFAQ, StackOverflow, man pages, POSIX, etc!\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1060)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "SC1061",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Ensure matching done for do in shell loops",
    "description" : "Detects missing 'done' keyword for a 'do' block in shell script loops, which causes syntax errors.",
    "explanation" : "## Couldn't find `done` for this `do`.\n\n### Problematic code:\n\n```sh\nyes() {\n  while echo \"y\"\n  do\n    true\n}\n```\n\n### Correct code:\n\n```sh\nyes() {\n  while echo \"y\"\n  do\n    true\n  done\n}\n```\n### Rationale:\n\nShellCheck found a `do` without a corresponding `done`.\n\nDouble check that the `done` exists, and that it correctly matches the indicated `do`. A companion warning [[SC1062]] is emitted where ShellCheck first noticed it was missing. \n\n### Exceptions:\n\nNone.\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1061)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "SC1062",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce matching 'done' for each 'do' in shell scripts",
    "description" : "This rule checks that every 'do' keyword in shell scripts is properly closed with a matching 'done'.",
    "explanation" : "## Expected `done` matching previously mentioned `do`.\n\nSee companion warning [[SC1061]]\n[Source](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck/wiki/SC1062)",
    "enabled" : false,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
